## 1. Line Plot

A line plot is useful for showing changes or trends in data.

Here, we will plot Passenger ID against Fare to visualize how the
fare values vary across passengers.

Code Cell 3

plt.plot(titanic["PassengerId"], titanic["Fare"])

plt.title("Passenger Fare by Passenger ID")
plt.xlabel("Passenger ID")
plt.ylabel("Fare")

plt.show()

The manual's example uses plt.plot() followed by title(), xlabel(), ylabel(), and show(). 

Markdown Cell 5

## 2. Scatter Plot

A scatter plot is used to show the relationship between two numerical
variables.

Here, we will compare Age and Fare for the passengers.

Code Cell 4

plt.scatter(titanic["Age"], titanic["Fare"])

plt.title("Age vs Fare")
plt.xlabel("Age")
plt.ylabel("Fare")

plt.show()

The manual describes a scatter plot as showing the relationship between two numerical variables. 

Markdown Cell 6

## 3. Bar Chart

A bar chart is useful for comparing different categories.

Here, we will count the number of passengers in each passenger class
and display the result using a bar chart.

Code Cell 5

class_counts = titanic["Pclass"].value_counts().sort_index()

plt.bar(
    class_counts.index.astype(str),
    class_counts.values
)

plt.title("Number of Passengers by Class")
plt.xlabel("Passenger Class")
plt.ylabel("Number of Passengers")

plt.show()

The manual states that a bar chart is useful for comparing categories. 

Markdown Cell 7

## 4. Histogram

A histogram shows the frequency distribution of numerical values.

Here, we will display the distribution of passenger ages.

Code Cell 6

plt.hist(titanic["Age"].dropna(), bins=10)

plt.title("Age Distribution of Passengers")
plt.xlabel("Age")
plt.ylabel("Frequency")

plt.show()

The manual specifically describes histograms as showing the frequency distribution of values. 

Markdown Cell 8

## 5. Pie Chart

A pie chart is useful for showing proportions of different categories.

Here, we will show the proportion of passengers who survived and
did not survive.

Code Cell 7

survival_counts = titanic["Survived"].value_counts()

plt.pie(
    survival_counts.values,
    labels=["Did Not Survive", "Survived"],
    autopct="%1.1f%%"
)

plt.title("Passenger Survival Distribution")

plt.show()

The manual gives a pie-chart example using labels and autopct to display percentages. 

Markdown Cell 9

## 6. Subplots

Subplots allow multiple graphs to be displayed within the same
figure.

In this example, we will create two plots:
1. A histogram showing Age distribution
2. A pie chart showing passenger survival

Code Cell 8

fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 5))

# Histogram
ax[0].hist(titanic["Age"].dropna(), bins=10)
ax[0].set_title("Age Distribution")
ax[0].set_xlabel("Age")
ax[0].set_ylabel("Frequency")

# Pie chart
survival_counts = titanic["Survived"].value_counts()

ax[1].pie(
    survival_counts.values,
    labels=["Did Not Survive", "Survived"],
    autopct="%1.1f%%"
)
ax[1].set_title("Survival Distribution")

plt.show()

The manual demonstrates using plt.subplots(1, 2) to place multiple plots in one figure. 

